# The LPG Reforms of 1991: Liberalisation, Privatisation, and Globalisation

> In June 1991 India was weeks from default, its gold flown abroad as
> collateral. The response — the New Economic Policy of July 1991 —
> dismantled four decades of controls under three watchwords:
> Liberalisation, Privatisation, and Globalisation. This lesson
> unpacks what each pillar contained and what the reforms delivered.

## 1. The Crisis of 1991: A Perfect Storm

By mid-1991 the Indian state faced a balance-of-payments crisis built
from several shocks at once:

- **Foreign exchange reserves collapsed** to about **US$1.2 billion**
  — barely two to three weeks of imports — while short-term foreign
  debt was falling due.
- The **Gulf War (1990-91)** tripled oil prices and choked remittances
  from Indian workers in the region as they returned home.
- Political drift of 1990-91 had borrowed abroad to cover deficits;
  **inflation crossed about 13 per cent** (WPI) by mid-1991.
- Creditors grew nervous; Indian Airlines and Air India had to
  arrange insurance abroad, and importers struggled for letters of
  credit.

[example]
The most dramatic signature of the crisis: in May and July 1991 the
Reserve Bank **airlifted about 67 tonnes of gold** — roughly 20
tonnes to the Union Bank of Switzerland and about 47 tonnes pledged
to the Bank of England — to raise about US$400 million and service
debt. Images of gold leaving the country made the crisis visceral
for the public and gave the government political cover for reform.
[/example]

P. V. Narasimha Rao became Prime Minister in June 1991 and appointed
**Dr Manmohan Singh** Finance Minister. India negotiated a stand-by
arrangement with the IMF (about **US$2.2 billion**), and on **24 July
1991** announced the New Economic Policy (NEP) — the LPG package.

## 2. The Three Pillars of the New Economic Policy

@diagram tree id="nep-lpg" layout="tree" aria="New Economic Policy 1991 with its three pillars"
  "New Economic Policy 1991" -> "Liberalisation"
  "New Economic Policy 1991" -> "Privatisation"
  "New Economic Policy 1991" -> "Globalisation"
  "Liberalisation" -> "End industrial licensing; price freedom"
  "Privatisation" -> "Disinvestment of PSU equity; autonomy to PSUs"
  "Globalisation" -> "Rupee devaluation; tariff cuts; FDI and FII entry"
@/diagram

[definition]
**Liberalisation** = freeing private enterprise from government
controls. **Privatisation** = enlarging the role of the private
sector, including dilution of government ownership of PSUs.
**Globalisation** = integrating the Indian economy with world markets
through trade, investment, and technology flows.
[/definition]

## 3. Liberalisation: Freeing Industry from the Licence Raj

The Industrial Policy Statement of 24 July 1991 **abolished
industrial licensing for all but 18 industries** — later trimmed to
15, then 9; today only a handful of strategic lines (liquor,
tobacco, defence, aerospace, explosives, and hazardous chemicals)
still need licences. The changes cascaded:

- **De-reservation**: industries once reserved for the public sector
  were thrown open; only atomic energy (and, for a while, railways)
  stayed reserved.
- **MRTP Act relaxed**: the threshold that required big houses to
  seek clearance for expansion was scrapped, letting large firms grow.
- **Small-scale reservation** began a gradual roll-back, and
  **freedom to set prices and choose technology** became the norm.
- Location restrictions were eased so industry could expand outside
  designated backward districts.

[warning]
Liberalisation did not mean *no* government — it meant replacing
ex-ante *permission* with ex-post *regulation*. Licensing moved from
the industry ministry's office to regulators: SEBI for markets,
TRAI for telecom (1997), CERC for power. Students often lose marks
for equating liberalisation with absence of rules.
[/warning]

## 4. Privatisation: Retreating from the Commanding Heights

The government began **disinvestment** — selling minority stakes in
public enterprises — in 1991-92, offloading about 8-10 per cent of
equity in about 40 PSUs and raising roughly ₹3,000 crore over the
first two years. A dedicated Disinvestment Commission (1996) and, in
1999, a Department of Disinvestment institutionalised the process.

| Stage | Policy | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1991-92 | Minority (8-10%) stake sales to institutions | 40 PSUs; no management change |
| 1993 onwards | Rangarajan Committee: up to 49% in reserved sectors | Widened dilution |
| 1997-2009 | Autonomy instead of sale: Navratna (1997), Miniratna, Maharatna (2009) | BHEL, ONGC, NTPC gained board powers |
| 2000s | Strategic sales with management transfer | Modern Foods, VSNL, BALCO, Hindustan Zinc |
| 2019 onwards | Strategic sector policy; big asset sales | Air India to TATA (2021-22) |

Notably, India's privatisation was selective: the state kept majority
control of most strategic PSUs, and autonomy packages ("ratnas") were
the more common reform — a distinction examiners frequently test.

## 5. Globalisation: Opening the Borders

- **Rupee devaluation, 1 and 3 July 1991**: two steps totalling about
  **18-19 per cent** against major currencies, restoring export
  competitiveness.
- **Trade policy**: the peak customs tariff — once above **300 per
  cent** — was cut in steps (150%, 110%, 85%…) to about 20 per cent
  by the early 2000s; quantitative import restrictions were phased
  out by 2001 under WTO commitments.
- **Investment**: FDI allowed up to **51 per cent in 34
  high-priority industries** (1991) through the automatic route;
  **Foreign Institutional Investors** admitted to Indian stock
  markets in 1992.
- **Convertibility**: the rupee became convertible on the **current
  account in August 1994** (IMF Article VIII); capital-account
  convertibility was deliberately not granted after the Tarapore
  Committee's caution.
- **Institutional**: India signed the **WTO agreement in 1994**
  (effective 1995), binding tariff and subsidy commitments.

[important]
Remember the sequence of the twin devaluations: about 9 per cent on
1 July 1991 and about 11 per cent on 3 July 1991 — cumulative
roughly 18-19 per cent, not 9 + 11 of the *base*, since the second
act applies to the already-devalued rate. Exams love this nuance.
[/important]

## 6. Financial-Sector and Tax Reforms

The crisis was also fiscal, so reform reached money and taxes:

- **Narasimham Committee (1991)** on banking: reduce pre-emptions.
  The **CRR fell from about 15 per cent to single digits** and the
  **SLR from 38.5 per cent to 25 per cent** over the 1990s, freeing
  bank funds for lending; interest rates were progressively
  deregulated.
- **SEBI Act, 1992** gave the market regulator statutory teeth; the
  **National Stock Exchange** began trading in 1994, breaking the
  BSE's monopoly with electronic screen-based dealing.
- **Chelliah Committee (1991-92)** on tax reform: broaden the base,
  cut rates, reduce evasion. Its lineage runs from MODVAT to
  state-level VAT (2005) and finally the **GST (2017)**.
- Exchange-rate regime moved to a market-determined (managed float)
  rate from March 1993.

## 7. Results: The Growth Dividend

The clearest outcome is visible in reserves, the very variable that
triggered the crisis:

@chart line title="India's foreign exchange reserves (US$ billion, approximate, year-end)" labels:
1991
1995
2000
2005
2010
2015
2020
2024

series:
  - name: "Forex reserves"
    data: 1.2 21 38 141 279 342 479 645
@/chart

- Growth stepped up: the **"Hindu rate of growth"** of about 3.5 per
  cent (1950s-1970s) gave way to 5-6 per cent in the 1990s and
  7-8 per cent in the mid-2000s boom.
- **Poverty fell** from about 45 per cent (1993-94) to about 22 per
  cent (2011-12) on the Tendulkar line — the fastest decline India
  had recorded.
- **Exports of services** (software, BPO) turned the current account
  less fragile; computer-services exports now run over US$150
  billion a year.
- Consumer choice exploded: colour televisions, two-wheelers, and
  telecom went from waiting lists to commodity abundance.

## 8. Criticisms: The Unevenness of Reform

[warning]
Reform was **asymmetric**: industry and finance were liberalised
quickly, agriculture slowly; the traded sector surged while farm
incomes grew with the weather; product markets opened before factor
markets (land, labour) did. The result, critics argue, was
**jobless-ish growth** — output rose faster than formal employment,
and the informal sector kept absorbing labour at low wages.
[/warning]

Other standard criticisms: disinvestment proceeds were sometimes
used to plug fiscal deficits rather than fund reform; small
producers faced import competition before infrastructure or credit
upgrades; and inequality rose alongside growth — the top decile's
income share climbed through the 2000s. Defenders reply that the
same period delivered the sharpest poverty decline in Indian
history, and that the 1991 framework survived later shocks (Asian
crisis 1997, sanctions 1998, global crisis 2008) that the pre-1991
economy was too rigid to withstand.

[tip]
Memory hook for the pillar-sequencing: **July 1991 = D-D-D-D** —
Devaluation (rupee), Disinvestment begins, Delicensing, and
Dismantling trade walls (tariff peak cut). SEBI Act '92, current
account '94, WTO '95.
[/tip]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Trigger year and package | 1991 crisis; New Economic Policy, 24 July 1991 |
| Forex reserves at the trough | About US$1.2 billion (2-3 weeks of imports) |
| Gold pledged | About 67 tonnes (UBS ~20 t; Bank of England ~47 t) |
| PM / Finance Minister | P. V. Narasimha Rao / Manmohan Singh |
| Licensing after 1991 | Abolished except 18 industries (later fewer) |
| Twin devaluations | 1 and 3 July 1991; about 18-19% combined |
| Peak tariff | From over 300% to about 20% by early 2000s |
| Banking reforms | Narasimham Committee 1991; CRR/SLR cut |
| Current account convertibility | August 1994 |
| Poverty decline | ~45% (1993-94) to ~22% (2011-12), Tendulkar line |
